  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-65000",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e7ae919f-4a7f-50e3-8c2f-7cea0b5a66ee",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Second Solution to a Differential Equation Using Reduction of Order",
      "problem": "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation.",
      "worked_solution": "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
