  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084956",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-129174",
      "knowledge_point_id": "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes",
      "problem": "Find a direction vector of the line that is given as the intersection of the planes $2x=6$ and $z=1-y.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that: \n\n- For the plane $2x=6$, a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,0,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $z=1-y$ (or $y+z=1$), a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨0,1,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,1,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-2j+2k \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨0,-2,2⟩",
              "option": "0",
              "source_option": "a"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨-2,2,0⟩",
              "option": "1",
              "source_option": "b"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨1,-2,2⟩",
              "option": "2",
              "source_option": "c"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨-2,1,2⟩",
              "option": "3",
              "source_option": "d"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "⟨2,-2,1⟩",
              "option": "4",
              "source_option": "e"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
